# Task 6 re-review — Fix Round 4

Date: 2026-08-28  
Review base: `1f3040d`  
Review head: `ae5d837`  
Disposition: **NEEDS FIXES**  
Open findings: **1 Critical, 3 Important, 1 Minor**

## Scope and method

This was a fresh read-only review of the complete Fix Round 4 section of
`task-6-report.md`, all 3,829 lines of `review-1f3040d..ae5d837.diff`, every
open finding in `task-6-rereview-3.md`, the Task 6 brief and approved design,
and the relevant current authorization, gateway, budget, audit, recovery, and
focused-test code. I did not rerun a test: static evidence was sufficient, and
the review constraint prohibited broad suites. No provider, network, live
pricing, real SDK, replay/parity, Docker, background process, permanent ledger,
or user authorization was used.

The threat model was calibrated as requested: untrusted model-authored code is
confined to the isolated policy worker and cannot import controller internals.
No finding relies on reflection, importing a private underscore name, or
mutating a private registry. Critical 1 uses only supported public Task 6
methods and public data contracts. Direct arbitrary corruption of the trusted
authorization ledger is not assumed.

Round 4 materially improves the normal path. The specialized provider writer
now requires a live gateway lifecycle; serialized receipts are cross-verified
against reopened audit evidence; reserved/start/terminal linkage and ordering
are substantially replayed; a genuine fresh BudgetLedger can restore; exact
terminal codes survive restart; and role snapshot bind/capture/consume is
synchronized. The implementation is still not ready because a second public
generic audit writer bypasses the lifecycle boundary, and restart replay can
mutate accounting before authorization, accept linked evidence, and ignore the
declared lifecycle state.

## Prior-finding adjudication

| Prior item | Round 4 verdict | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
